## Worked Example: A Small Permission System

This program combines arithmetic, bitwise flags, casts, and `<stdbool.h>` into a tiny permission-flag system, a realistic use of everything in this module:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdbool.h>

#define PERM_READ  (1 << 0)
#define PERM_WRITE (1 << 1)
#define PERM_EXEC  (1 << 2)

bool hasPermission(unsigned int perms, unsigned int flag) {
    return (perms & flag) != 0;
}

int main(void) {
    unsigned int filePerms = PERM_READ | PERM_WRITE;

    printf("can read?  %d\n", hasPermission(filePerms, PERM_READ));
    printf("can write? %d\n", hasPermission(filePerms, PERM_WRITE));
    printf("can exec?  %d\n", hasPermission(filePerms, PERM_EXEC));

    filePerms |= PERM_EXEC;          // grant execute
    filePerms &= ~PERM_WRITE;        // revoke write

    printf("after changes -> write:%d exec:%d\n",
           hasPermission(filePerms, PERM_WRITE),
           hasPermission(filePerms, PERM_EXEC));

    int totalFiles = 7, checked = 2;
    double fraction = (double)checked / totalFiles; // cast avoids truncation
    printf("checked fraction: %.2f\n", fraction);

    return 0;
}
```

Notice `hasPermission` returns `bool` (from `<stdbool.h>`) even though C prints it with `%d` as `0`/`1` — there's no dedicated boolean format specifier, because `bool` really is just a small integer underneath.

Also notice the order of operations matters here: `filePerms |= PERM_EXEC` and `filePerms &= ~PERM_WRITE` are each single, self-contained statements rather than combined into one expression that reads and writes `filePerms` multiple times — exactly the discipline recommended earlier for avoiding undefined evaluation-order bugs. Real permission systems (Unix file mode bits, hardware status registers) use precisely this set/test/clear vocabulary, just with more flags and often a whole byte or word's worth of them packed together.

## Recap

- Integer division truncates; cast an operand to `double` for a fractional result.
- Classic C has no boolean type — `0` is false, any nonzero value is true; `<stdbool.h>` (C99+) adds `bool`/`true`/`false` as a thin layer over `int`.
- `(Type)expr` performs an explicit cast; implicit "usual arithmetic conversions" happen automatically when mixing types.
- Comparing signed and unsigned integers silently converts the signed value to unsigned — a classic, hard-to-spot bug source.
- Modifying the same variable more than once in one expression without a sequence point in between is undefined behavior.
- `&&` and `||` short-circuit left-to-right — a common and safe idiom for guarding pointer dereferences.
- Never compare floating point values with `==`; use a small epsilon tolerance instead.

codeExamples.code:
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int a = 7, b = 2;
    printf("%d\n", a / b);               // 3
    printf("%f\n", (double)a / b);       // 3.500000

    int flag = 5;
    if (flag) printf("nonzero is true\n"); // any nonzero is truthy
    return 0;
}

#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int a = -1;
    unsigned int b = 1;
    if (a < b) {
        printf("a is smaller\n");
    } else {
        printf("a converted to a huge unsigned value!\n");
    }
    return 0;
}

#include <stdio.h>

#define FLAG_READ  (1 << 0)
#define FLAG_WRITE (1 << 1)

int main(void) {
    unsigned int perms = FLAG_READ;
    perms |= FLAG_WRITE;               // set
    printf("write? %d\n", (perms & FLAG_WRITE) != 0); // test
    perms &= ~FLAG_WRITE;               // clear
    printf("write? %d\n", (perms & FLAG_WRITE) != 0);
    return 0;
}

