## Three Standard Streams

Every C program starts with three open I/O streams, declared in `<stdio.h>`: `stdin` (keyboard/input), `stdout` (normal output), and `stderr` (error output, unbuffered and separate so error messages aren't lost if `stdout` is redirected to a file). `printf` writes to `stdout`; `fprintf(stderr, ...)` writes to `stderr`.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    fprintf(stdout, "normal output\n");
    fprintf(stderr, "something went wrong\n");
    return 0;
}
```

## `printf`: Formatted Output

`printf` takes a format string containing literal text plus `%`-prefixed specifiers, followed by one argument per specifier:

```c
int age = 30;
double gpa = 3.75;
printf("Age %d, GPA %.2f\n", age, gpa);
```

Common specifiers: `%d` (int), `%ld` (long), `%f`/`%.2f` (double, with precision), `%c` (char), `%s` (string), `%p` (pointer), `%%` (a literal percent sign).

## `scanf`: Formatted Input

`scanf` parses `stdin` according to a format string and writes results into variables — but it needs the **address** of each variable (`&x`), because it must write into your variable's memory rather than receive a copy of it:

```c
int age;
printf("Enter age: ");
scanf("%d", &age);
printf("Next year you'll be %d\n", age + 1);
```

Forgetting the `&` (a very common beginner mistake) passes `scanf` the *value* of `age` instead of its address — it then tries to write through whatever garbage integer that value happens to represent as a memory address, which is undefined behavior and often crashes.

## Checking `scanf`'s Return Value

`scanf` returns the **number of items successfully matched and assigned** — not a boolean. Real code checks it:

```c
int n;
if (scanf("%d", &n) != 1) {
    fprintf(stderr, "invalid input\n");
    return 1;
}
```

If the user types letters where a number was expected, `scanf` matches zero items, leaves `n` uninitialized, and — critically — leaves the bad input still sitting unread in the input buffer, ready to break the *next* `scanf` call too.

## Never Use `gets()`

The old `gets()` function (reads a line with no length limit) was so dangerous — it cannot be given a buffer size, so any input longer than the buffer overflows it — that it was **removed from the C standard** in C11. Never use it; it appears only in very old legacy code and textbooks.

## Prefer `fgets` + `sscanf` Over Raw `scanf`

The idiomatic, safer pattern for interactive input is to read a whole line with a bounded size using `fgets`, then parse it with `sscanf`:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

char line[128];
if (fgets(line, sizeof(line), stdin) != NULL) {
    int n;
    if (sscanf(line, "%d", &n) == 1) {
        printf("got %d\n", n);
    } else {
        printf("not a number\n");
    }
}
```

`fgets` always respects the buffer size you give it (`sizeof(line)`), so it can never overflow the buffer — unlike bare `scanf("%s", ...)`. It also keeps the trailing `\n` in the buffer (worth stripping if you don't want it):

```c
size_t len = strlen(line);
if (len > 0 && line[len - 1] == '\n') {
    line[len - 1] = '\0';
}
```

## Leftover Newlines Between Mixed Reads

Mixing `scanf("%d", ...)` with a later `scanf("%c", ...)` or `fgets` is the classic trap: the newline from pressing Enter after typing a number is left unconsumed and immediately satisfies the very next read. Two common fixes: use a leading space in the format (`" %c"`, which skips *any* whitespace including newlines), or explicitly drain the rest of the line:

```c
int c;
while ((c = getchar()) != '\n' && c != EOF) { /* discard */ }
```

## Always Check Return Values

Both `scanf` and `fgets` communicate failure through their return value — `scanf` returns the count of successfully matched items, `fgets` returns `NULL` on end-of-file or error. Production-quality C treats every one of these return values as something to check, not something to assume:

```c
if (fgets(line, sizeof(line), stdin) == NULL) {
    fprintf(stderr, "no input received\n");
}
```

Skipping these checks is one of the most common ways beginner (and sometimes professional) C programs misbehave on unexpected input.

## Worked Example: Robust Number Averaging

This program reads a count, then that many numbers, validating every step — a realistic combination of everything covered in this module:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

int main(void) {
    char line[128];
    int count;

    printf("How many numbers? ");
    if (fgets(line, sizeof(line), stdin) == NULL ||
        sscanf(line, "%d", &count) != 1 || count <= 0) {
        fprintf(stderr, "invalid count\n");
        return 1;
    }

    double sum = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < count; i++) {
        printf("Number %d: ", i + 1);
        if (fgets(line, sizeof(line), stdin) == NULL) {
            fprintf(stderr, "unexpected end of input\n");
            return 1;
        }

        double value;
        if (sscanf(line, "%lf", &value) != 1) {
            fprintf(stderr, "not a number, skipping\n");
            i--; // retry this slot
            continue;
        }
        sum += value;
    }

    printf("Average: %.2f\n", sum / count);
    return 0;
}
```

Every read goes through `fgets` first (bounded, safe, never overflows), then `sscanf` parses the specific value out of the line — the idiomatic replacement for scattering raw `scanf` calls throughout the program. Notice the `i--; continue;` retry logic on a bad number: because the loop tracks progress with an explicit counter rather than trusting `scanf` to "just work," one malformed line doesn't derail the rest of the input — it simply asks again for that same slot, which is exactly the kind of resilience real interactive programs need from user-facing input loops.

## Recap

- `stdin`/`stdout`/`stderr` are the three standard streams every C program starts with.
- `scanf` needs the address of each variable (`&var`) to write into it, and returns the number of successfully matched items — always check it.
- `scanf`'s `%c` doesn't skip whitespace like other specifiers do — leftover newlines are a classic trap; a leading space (`" %c"`) fixes it.
- `gets()` is unsafe and was removed from the standard; never use it.
- The idiomatic, safe input pattern is `fgets` (bounded line read) followed by `sscanf` (parsing).
- Streams are buffered; `fflush(stdout)` forces pending output out immediately when needed.

codeExamples.code:
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int age;
    printf("Enter age: ");
    scanf("%d", &age);
    printf("Next year you'll be %d\n", age + 1);
    return 0;
}

#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int n;
    char c;

    printf("Enter a number: ");
    scanf("%d", &n);

    printf("Enter a letter: ");
    scanf(" %c", &c);  // leading space skips the leftover newline

    printf("n=%d c=%c\n", n, c);
    return 0;
}

#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    char line[64];
    printf("Enter your age: ");
    if (fgets(line, sizeof(line), stdin) != NULL) {
        int age;
        if (sscanf(line, "%d", &age) == 1) {
            printf("You are %d\n", age);
        } else {
            printf("That wasn't a number.\n");
        }
    }
    return 0;
}

