## The Original Control Structures

`if/else if/else`, `for`, `while`, `do-while`, and `switch` in C are the exact syntax every later C-family language (Java, C++, C#, JavaScript) copied. Learning them here means recognizing them everywhere else.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int score = 82;

    if (score >= 90) {
        printf("A\n");
    } else if (score >= 80) {
        printf("B\n");
    } else {
        printf("C or below\n");
    }
    return 0;
}
```

## The Three Loop Forms

`for` bundles initialization, condition, and increment into one line — ideal when you know the iteration count in advance:

```c
for (int i = 1; i <= 10; i++) {
    if (i % 2 == 0) continue; // skip even numbers
    printf("%d ", i);
}
printf("\n");
```

`while` checks its condition before each iteration (may run zero times); `do-while` checks *after* the first iteration (always runs at least once):

```c
int n = 5;
while (n > 0) { printf("%d ", n); n--; }   // 5 4 3 2 1

int input;
do {
    printf("Enter a positive number: ");
    scanf("%d", &input);
} while (input <= 0); // guarantees at least one prompt
```

## `switch` and Fall-Through

`switch` jumps to the matching `case` label and then **keeps executing every subsequent case** until it hits a `break` or the end of the `switch` block — this "fall-through" behavior is intentional (occasionally useful for grouping cases) but a very common source of bugs when a `break` is forgotten:

```c
int day = 3;
switch (day) {
    case 1: printf("Mon"); break;
    case 2: printf("Tue"); break;
    case 3: printf("Wed"); break;
    default: printf("Unknown");
}
```

Without the `break` after `case 3`, execution would continue printing `default` too.

## Truthiness in Conditions

Just like in expressions generally, `if`/`while` treat any nonzero value as true and zero as false — there's no requirement that the condition be a "real" boolean:

```c
int remaining = 3;
while (remaining) {   // equivalent to remaining != 0
    printf("%d\n", remaining--);
}
```

## Under the Hood: Jumps and Labels

At the machine-code level, there's no such thing as `if`, `for`, or `switch` — the compiler translates all of them into **conditional and unconditional jump instructions** operating on a program counter. An `if/else` becomes "compare, jump-if-false to the else label, otherwise fall through, then jump-past-else at the end." A `for` loop desugars into essentially a `while` with the increment folded in:

```c
for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    printf("%d\n", i);
}

/* is roughly equivalent to: */
int i = 0;
while (i < 5) {
    printf("%d\n", i);
    i++;
}
```

Understanding this equivalence explains why `continue` in a `for` loop still runs the increment (it jumps to the increment step, not past it) — a frequent point of confusion for beginners moving from `while` to `for`.

## `switch` as a Jump Table

When `case` values are small and densely packed, compilers often implement `switch` as a **jump table** — an array of code addresses indexed directly by the switch value — making it O(1) rather than a chain of comparisons. This is one reason `switch` on the same variable can be faster than an equivalent `if/else if` chain with many branches, though modern compilers optimize both fairly well.

## The Dangling `else`

An `else` always binds to the **nearest unmatched `if`**, which can produce surprising results with nested `if`s and no braces:

```c
if (a > 0)
    if (b > 0)
        printf("both positive\n");
else
    printf("a is not positive\n");  // actually binds to the inner if!
```

Despite the indentation suggesting otherwise, that `else` pairs with `if (b > 0)`, not `if (a > 0)` — the compiler doesn't care about whitespace. Always use braces `{}` around `if` bodies once there's any nesting, purely to make the binding visually and structurally unambiguous.

## `switch` Requires Integral Types

`switch` operates only on integer (or enum) expressions — `case` labels must be *compile-time constant* integers, and you cannot `switch` on a `double` or a `char*` string directly. Comparing strings requires an `if/else if` chain with `strcmp` instead.

## Infinite Loops Are a Deliberate Idiom

`for (;;) { ... }` and `while (1) { ... }` are common, intentional idioms for "loop until something inside explicitly breaks," frequently seen in event loops and servers:

```c
for (;;) {
    int event = waitForEvent();
    if (event == EVENT_QUIT) break;
    handleEvent(event);
}
```

## `break` and `continue`

`break` exits the nearest enclosing loop or `switch` immediately; `continue` skips the rest of the current iteration and proceeds to the next one (running the `for` loop's increment step first, if applicable):

```c
for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++) {
    if (i % 15 == 0 && i != 0) break;    // stop early
    if (i % 3 == 0) continue;             // skip multiples of 3
    printf("%d ", i);
}
```

## `goto` — Rare, But One Legitimate Idiom

`goto` is generally discouraged for ordinary control flow (it easily produces unreadable "spaghetti code"), but C has **one widely accepted idiom** where it genuinely is the cleanest tool: centralized cleanup on error paths, since C has no exceptions or `finally` block:

```c
int process(void) {
    FILE *f = NULL;
    char *buf = NULL;
    int result = -1;

    f = fopen("data.txt", "r");
    if (f == NULL) goto cleanup;

    buf = malloc(1024);
    if (buf == NULL) goto cleanup;

    /* ... use f and buf ... */
    result = 0;

cleanup:
    if (buf) free(buf);
    if (f) fclose(f);
    return result;
}
```

This pattern avoids duplicating cleanup code at every possible early-return point — a real problem once a function acquires several resources that all need releasing on any failure path. This is covered further in the error handling module.

## Common Pitfalls

- **Forgetting `break` in a `switch`.** Always add it explicitly (or comment `/* fall through */` if fall-through is genuinely intended, which most compilers recognize to suppress warnings).
- **The dangling `else`.** Brace every `if` body once there's nested conditional logic, even for single statements.
- **Off-by-one loop bounds.** `for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++)` iterates `n + 1` times, not `n` — a frequent source of one-past-the-end array accesses. Double check `<` vs `<=` against what you actually want.
- **Modifying a loop's index inside the body unexpectedly.** Changing `i` inside a `for` loop body interacts confusingly with the loop's own increment; avoid it unless it's a deliberate, well-commented technique.
- **Infinite loops from a wrong condition.** `while (x = 5)` (assignment, not comparison) always evaluates truthy — a classic typo. Many compilers warn on this (`-Wall` flags assignment-in-condition); consider writing `while (5 == x)` to make the mismatch a compile error if you truly mistype `=`.

## Worked Example: A Simple State Machine

Control flow constructs combine naturally to build a small traffic-light state machine — a common real-world pattern for `switch`, loops, and `break`:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

typedef enum { RED, GREEN, YELLOW } Light;

const char *lightName(Light l) {
    switch (l) {
        case RED:    return "RED";
        case GREEN:  return "GREEN";
        case YELLOW: return "YELLOW";
        default:     return "UNKNOWN";
    }
}

Light next(Light l) {
    switch (l) {
        case RED:    return GREEN;
        case GREEN:  return YELLOW;
        case YELLOW: return RED;
        default:     return RED;
    }
}

int main(void) {
    Light current = RED;

    for (int step = 0; step < 6; step++) {
        printf("Step %d: %s\n", step, lightName(current));
        current = next(current);
    }

    /* Simulate an emergency override loop */
    int emergency = 0;
    while (1) {
        if (emergency) {
            printf("Emergency override -> RED\n");
            break;
        }
        emergency = 1; // pretend an event triggers this
    }

    return 0;
}
```

This demonstrates `switch` used both as a lookup ("name for this state") and a transition table ("what state comes next"), plus an intentional `while (1)` loop broken out of explicitly — exactly the shape of a real embedded or game-loop state machine. Notice that both `lightName` and `next` end their `switch` with a `default` case even though, logically, every enum value is already handled — this is standard defensive practice, since an out-of-range value could still reach the function (e.g. a corrupted variable, or a future enum value someone forgets to add a case for), and a `default` gives you a controlled fallback instead of undefined behavior falling off the end of the switch.

## Recap

- `for`/`while`/`do-while`/`switch` in C are the template every later C-family language followed.
- `switch` falls through between cases unless each ends in `break` — a deliberate but easy-to-misuse feature.
- Under the hood, all control flow compiles down to conditional/unconditional jumps; `for` is essentially sugar over `while`.
- The dangling-`else` rule means `else` always binds to the nearest unmatched `if` — brace bodies to avoid ambiguity.
- `goto` is discouraged generally, but is a legitimate, idiomatic tool for centralized error-path cleanup.
- Off-by-one bounds (`<` vs `<=`) and forgotten `break`s are the two most common control-flow bugs in real C code.

codeExamples.code:
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    for (int i = 1; i <= 10; i++) {
        if (i % 2 == 0) continue;
        printf("%d ", i);
    }
    printf("\n");

    int day = 3;
    switch (day) {
        case 1: printf("Mon"); break;
        case 2: printf("Tue"); break;
        case 3: printf("Wed"); break;
        default: printf("Unknown");
    }
    return 0;
}

#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int process(void) {
    char *buf = NULL;
    FILE *f = NULL;
    int result = -1;

    f = fopen("data.txt", "r");
    if (!f) goto cleanup;

    buf = malloc(64);
    if (!buf) goto cleanup;

    result = 0; // success

cleanup:
    if (buf) free(buf);
    if (f) fclose(f);
    return result;
}

int main(void) {
    printf("result = %d\n", process());
    return 0;
}

#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int a = 1, b = 0;

    if (a > 0)
        if (b > 0)
            printf("both positive\n");
        else
            printf("a positive, b not (binds to inner if)\n");

    return 0;
}

