## Anatomy of a Function

A C function has a return type, a name, a parameter list, and a body:

```c
int add(int a, int b) {
    return a + b;
}
```

`void` as a return type means "returns nothing"; `void` as the *only* parameter (`int f(void)`) explicitly means "takes no arguments" (an empty `()` in old-style C actually meant "unspecified arguments," which is why modern code writes `(void)` for zero-parameter functions).

## Prototypes and Declaration Order

C compiles top-to-bottom in a single pass per file, so a function must be **declared** before it's used — either by defining it earlier in the file, or by giving a **prototype** (the signature without a body) before `main`:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int square(int n); // prototype — declares the signature ahead of use

int main(void) {
    printf("%d\n", square(5));
    return 0;
}

int square(int n) { // full definition, can come after main
    return n * n;
}
```

Without the prototype, calling `square` before its definition either fails to compile (in modern C) or, in very old C, assumes it returns `int` and accepts whatever arguments you passed — a source of hard-to-diagnose bugs.

## Pass-by-Value: the Core Rule

C is **strictly pass-by-value**: every argument is *copied* into the function's parameter. Changes made to a parameter inside the function never affect the caller's original variable:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

void incByValue(int x) { x++; }

int main(void) {
    int a = 5;
    incByValue(a);
    printf("%d\n", a); // 5 — unchanged, because a was copied
    return 0;
}
```

## Mutating the Caller: Pass a Pointer

To let a function modify a caller's variable, pass a **pointer** to it, and dereference (`*x`) inside the function to read/write through that address:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

void incByPointer(int *x) { (*x)++; }

int main(void) {
    int a = 5;
    incByPointer(&a);
    printf("%d\n", a); // 6 — changed, because we wrote through its address
    return 0;
}
```

This single rule — "C never lets a function change your variable unless you hand it an address" — is one of the most important mental models in the whole language, and it explains why so many standard library functions (`scanf`, and plenty you'll write yourself) take pointer parameters.

## The Call Stack

Each function call pushes a new **stack frame** holding its parameters, local variables, and a return address. When the function returns, that frame is popped and its memory is reclaimed — which is exactly why returning a pointer to a local variable is a serious bug:

```c
int *dangerous(void) {
    int local = 42;
    return &local; // BUG: local's stack frame is gone after return
}
```

The returned pointer refers to memory that's no longer reserved for anything — reading through it afterward is undefined behavior, even though it might "seem to work" by luck.

## Recursion and Stack Overflow

Recursive functions consume one stack frame per call-in-progress. Deep or unbounded recursion exhausts the stack, crashing the program (a **stack overflow**, distinct from a heap buffer overflow):

```c
long factorial(int n) {
    if (n <= 1) return 1;
    return n * factorial(n - 1); // one frame per pending multiplication
}
```

Every recursive function needs a **base case** that stops the recursion; C provides no automatic tail-call optimization guarantee, so very deep recursion (tens of thousands of levels) is genuinely risky.

## Arrays "Decay" When Passed to Functions

When you pass an array to a function, C does **not** copy the whole array — the array expression *decays* into a pointer to its first element. Inside the function, `sizeof` on that parameter gives the pointer's size, not the original array's:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

void printSize(int arr[]) {
    printf("%zu\n", sizeof(arr)); // size of a pointer (e.g. 8), NOT the array!
}

int main(void) {
    int nums[10];
    printf("%zu\n", sizeof(nums)); // 40 (10 ints) — the real array size
    printSize(nums);
    return 0;
}
```

Because the size information is lost, functions that operate on arrays conventionally take an explicit length parameter too: `void process(int *arr, size_t length)`.

## Storage Classes: `static`, `extern`, `auto`

- **`auto`** (the default, rarely written explicitly) — ordinary local variable, lives on the stack, destroyed at block exit.
- **`static`** on a local variable — persists its value **between calls**, initialized only once:

```c
int counter(void) {
    static int calls = 0; // initialized once, retains value across calls
    return ++calls;
}
```

- **`static`** on a file-scope function/variable — restricts visibility to that translation unit (`.c` file) only, a form of encapsulation covered more in the idioms page.
- **`extern`** — declares that a variable/function is defined in *another* file, used to share globals across multiple `.c` files via a shared header.

## Prototypes Belong in Headers

In multi-file C programs, function prototypes live in a shared `.h` header, `#include`d by any `.c` file that needs to call them — this is how C achieves separate compilation while still letting the compiler check call signatures.

```c
/* math_utils.h */
int square(int n);

/* math_utils.c */
#include "math_utils.h"
int square(int n) { return n * n; }

/* main.c */
#include "math_utils.h"
int main(void) { return square(5); }
```

## `static` Functions for Internal Linkage

Marking a function `static` at file scope hides it from other `.c` files — it becomes a private implementation detail of that translation unit, the closest C gets to "private" methods:

```c
static int helper(int x) { return x * 2; } // only callable within this file
int publicApi(int x) { return helper(x) + 1; }
```

This is idiomatic for internal helpers you don't want polluting the global namespace or being called (and depended on) from elsewhere.

## Function Pointers: Passing Behavior, Not Just Data

A function's name, used without calling it, decays to a **function pointer** — letting you pass behavior as a parameter (callbacks), the same role first-class functions play in other languages:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }
int mul(int a, int b) { return a * b; }

int apply(int a, int b, int (*op)(int, int)) {
    return op(a, b);
}

int main(void) {
    printf("%d\n", apply(3, 4, add)); // 7
    printf("%d\n", apply(3, 4, mul)); // 12
    return 0;
}
```

`int (*op)(int, int)` reads as "`op` is a pointer to a function taking two `int`s and returning `int`." This idiom underlies qsort's comparator argument, GUI callbacks, and the struct-embedded "methods" covered in the structs module.

## `const` Parameters Document Intent

Marking a pointer parameter `const` promises callers the function won't modify what it points to — the compiler enforces it:

```c
void printArray(const int *arr, size_t len) {
    for (size_t i = 0; i < len; i++) printf("%d ", arr[i]);
}
```

## Common Pitfalls

- **Returning a pointer to a local variable** — always a bug; return by value, use `static`/heap allocation, or have the caller supply the buffer instead.
- **Forgetting array parameters lose their size** — always pass length alongside a raw pointer/array parameter.
- **Deep unbounded recursion** — convert to iteration, or ensure a provably shallow bound, for anything processing untrusted-size input.
- **Assuming pass-by-value applies to arrays** — it doesn't; arrays are always effectively passed "by reference" (as a pointer) since they decay, which surprises people used to structs (which *are* copied by value).

## Worked Example: Recursion, Pointers, and Callbacks Together

This program combines a recursive function, a pointer parameter used to return an extra value, and a function pointer callback:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

long factorial(int n) {
    if (n <= 1) return 1;
    return n * (long)factorial(n - 1);
}

/* Returns quotient, writes remainder through a pointer parameter --
   a common way to "return" more than one value from a C function. */
int divmod(int a, int b, int *remainder) {
    *remainder = a % b;
    return a / b;
}

void forEach(const int *arr, size_t len, void (*visit)(int)) {
    for (size_t i = 0; i < len; i++) {
        visit(arr[i]);
    }
}

void printDouble(int x) {
    printf("%d ", x * 2);
}

int main(void) {
    printf("5! = %ld\n", factorial(5));

    int rem;
    int quot = divmod(17, 5, &rem);
    printf("17 / 5 = %d remainder %d\n", quot, rem);

    int values[] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
    forEach(values, 5, printDouble);
    printf("\n");

    return 0;
}
```

`divmod` demonstrates the very common C idiom of "return the main result normally, return secondary results through pointer parameters" — since a C function can only truly `return` one value directly.

## Recap

- C is always pass-by-value — arguments are copied into the function's parameters; pointers are how you opt into mutation.
- A function must be declared (a prototype, typically in a header) before it's called, unless it's defined earlier in the same file.
- Each call gets its own stack frame; never return a pointer to a local variable — its storage is gone once the function returns.
- Arrays decay to pointers when passed to functions, losing size information — pass the length explicitly alongside.
- `static` locals persist across calls; `static` functions/globals restrict visibility to their own file for internal-only helpers.
- Function pointers let you pass behavior as a value — the foundation of callbacks and, later, struct-based "polymorphism."
- Recursion needs a base case and a bounded depth — C has no guaranteed tail-call optimization.

