## Fixed-Size, Contiguous Memory

An array is a fixed-size block of same-typed elements laid out contiguously in memory. Its size is fixed at declaration (or by the initializer) and cannot grow:

```c
int nums[5] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    printf("%d ", nums[i]);
}
printf("\n");
```

`nums[i]` is **defined** in the C standard as shorthand for `*(nums + i)` — indexing is really pointer arithmetic on the array's starting address, scaled automatically by the size of one element. This equivalence is exact, not just a mental model — `i[nums]` (reversed) actually compiles and works identically, though nobody should ever write that.

## There Is No Built-In String Type

C has no dedicated string type. A "string" is simply a `char` array terminated by a null byte, `'\0'` (numeric value 0), which every string-handling function uses to know where the text ends:

```c
char greeting[6] = {'H', 'e', 'l', 'l', 'o', '\0'};
/* equivalently, and far more commonly: */
char greeting2[] = "Hello"; // the compiler appends '\0' automatically
```

Note `"Hello"` needs 6 bytes of storage, not 5 — the terminator counts.

## `<string.h>`: the Core String Toolkit

```c
#include <string.h>

char greeting[32] = "Hello";
strcat(greeting, ", world");           // append (buffer must have room!)
printf("%s (len=%zu)\n", greeting, strlen(greeting));

char a[] = "abc", b[] = "abc";
printf("%d\n", strcmp(a, b) == 0);      // 1 — equal strings compare as 0

char dest[16];
strcpy(dest, "hi there");               // copy into dest
```

`strlen` returns the length **excluding** the null terminator; `strcmp` returns `0` for equality (not `true`/`1`), and negative/positive values indicating lexicographic order otherwise — a common source of `if (strcmp(a, b))` bugs when people forget it's not a boolean "are these equal" function.

## Buffer Sizes Are Entirely Your Responsibility

None of `strcat`, `strcpy`, or writing to an array index checks whether the destination has enough room. `greeting[32]` must actually be big enough to hold `"Hello, world"` plus its null terminator (13 bytes) — C will not stop you from writing past the end, it will simply corrupt adjacent memory.

## Prefer Bounded Functions

`strcpy`/`strcat`/`sprintf`/`scanf("%s", ...)` have no notion of the destination buffer's size and will overflow it silently given long-enough input. Prefer the bounded variants, and *always* check that they actually null-terminate (some do, some don't, in edge cases):

```c
char dest[16];
strncpy(dest, source, sizeof(dest) - 1);
dest[sizeof(dest) - 1] = '\0'; // strncpy does not guarantee null-termination
                                 // if source is >= the given length

snprintf(dest, sizeof(dest), "%s, %s", first, last); // always null-terminates
```

`snprintf` is generally the more reliably safe choice of the two — it always null-terminates (truncating if necessary) and reports how many characters *would* have been written, letting you detect truncation.

## Iterating and Off-By-One Errors

The most common string bug is an **off-by-one**: forgetting the null terminator when sizing a buffer, or looping one element too far/short:

```c
char name[5] = "Anna"; // exactly fits: 4 letters + '\0' — no room to grow
strcat(name, "!");      // BUG: overflows name by writing past its end
```

`name` needed to be declared with at least one extra byte of slack for any future appending.

## `strtok` Mutates Its Input and Keeps Hidden State

`strtok` splits a string on delimiters, but it **writes null bytes directly into the string you pass it** (so it must be a mutable buffer, not a string literal), and it keeps internal state between calls (making it non-reentrant/not thread-safe) — call it repeatedly with `NULL` as the first argument to continue tokenizing the same string:

```c
char text[] = "one,two,three";
char *token = strtok(text, ",");
while (token != NULL) {
    printf("%s\n", token);
    token = strtok(NULL, ","); // NULL means "continue from where we left off"
}
```

`strtok_r` is the reentrant, thread-safe alternative, taking an explicit state pointer.

## Building Strings Dynamically

When a string's final size isn't known up front, allocate it on the heap (covered in depth in the memory management module) rather than guessing at a fixed buffer size:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

char *combine(const char *a, const char *b) {
    size_t len = strlen(a) + strlen(b) + 1; // +1 for '\0'
    char *result = malloc(len);
    if (result == NULL) return NULL;
    snprintf(result, len, "%s%s", a, b);
    return result;
}
```

The caller of `combine` is responsible for eventually calling `free()` on the returned pointer — ownership of heap memory always needs a clear, documented convention.

## Worked Example: A Tiny Word Counter

This program combines array iteration, string functions, and careful bounds handling to count words in a sentence — a realistic small text-processing task:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

int countWords(const char *s) {
    int count = 0;
    int inWord = 0;

    for (size_t i = 0; s[i] != '\0'; i++) {
        if (s[i] == ' ' || s[i] == '\t' || s[i] == '\n') {
            inWord = 0;
        } else if (!inWord) {
            inWord = 1;
            count++;
        }
    }
    return count;
}

void reverseString(char *s) {
    size_t len = strlen(s);
    for (size_t i = 0, j = len - 1; i < j; i++, j--) {
        char tmp = s[i];
        s[i] = s[j];
        s[j] = tmp;
    }
}

int main(void) {
    char sentence[] = "the quick brown fox jumps";
    printf("Word count: %d\n", countWords(sentence));

    char word[] = "hello";
    reverseString(word);
    printf("Reversed: %s\n", word);

    char buffer[64];
    snprintf(buffer, sizeof(buffer), "%s has %d words", sentence, countWords(sentence));
    printf("%s\n", buffer);

    return 0;
}
```

`countWords` walks the string once, tracking whether it's currently "inside" a word — an idiom that generalizes to tokenizing, parsing, and lexing tasks throughout C.

## Recap

- `arr[i]` is exactly equivalent to `*(arr + i)` — arrays and pointer arithmetic are the same underlying mechanism.
- A C string is a `char` array ending in a `'\0'` null terminator; the terminator counts toward required buffer size.
- Arrays decay to pointers when passed to functions, losing their `sizeof` information — pass length explicitly.
- String literals (`"..."`) are read-only; only `char arr[] = "..."` gives you a mutable, writable copy.
- `strlen`/`strcpy`/`strcmp`/`strcat` (`string.h`) operate on null-terminated char arrays and trust you to manage buffer sizes.
- Prefer bounded functions (`snprintf`, `strncpy` with manual null-termination) over `strcpy`/`strcat`/`sprintf` to avoid overflow.
- `strtok` mutates its input and keeps hidden state between calls — pass `NULL` to continue tokenizing the same string.

