## Grouping Related Data

C has no classes and no built-in object-oriented programming, but `struct` lets you bundle related fields into a single named type — the foundation every OO language's "class" eventually builds on:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

struct Rectangle {
    int width;
    int height;
};

int main(void) {
    struct Rectangle r = {3, 4};   // members in declaration order
    printf("%d\n", r.width * r.height);
    return 0;
}
```

Members are accessed with the dot operator (`r.width`), and can be initialized in order (`{3, 4}`) or by name (**designated initializers**, C99+): `struct Rectangle r = {.width = 3, .height = 4};` — the named form is clearer and safer when a struct has many fields, since it doesn't depend on remembering declaration order.

## `typedef` Removes the `struct` Keyword Repetition

Without `typedef`, every declaration needs the `struct` keyword: `struct Rectangle r;`. `typedef` lets you introduce a plain-looking type name instead:

```c
typedef struct {
    int width;
    int height;
} Rectangle;   // now "Rectangle" alone is a valid type name

Rectangle r = {3, 4};
```

This anonymous-struct-plus-typedef pattern (`typedef struct { ... } Name;`) is the standard idiom in modern C for defining a struct type — you'll see it in essentially every real C codebase.

## Structs Hold Data Only — "Methods" Are Free Functions

There is no method syntax attached to a struct. Instead, ordinary functions take the struct (or a pointer to it) as an explicit first parameter, simulating the "object receives a message" idea by convention rather than language support:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

typedef struct {
    int width;
    int height;
} Rectangle;

int area(Rectangle r) {
    return r.width * r.height;
}

int main(void) {
    Rectangle r = {3, 4};
    printf("%d\n", area(r));
    return 0;
}
```

This is exactly the framing C's absence of OOP replaces: where C++/Java would write `r.area()`, C writes `area(r)` — the data and the logic that operates on it are separate, connected only by naming convention and the header file that declares both together.

## Structs Occupy Contiguous Memory — With Padding

A struct's members are laid out in memory in declaration order, but the compiler may insert **padding** bytes between them so each member starts at an address matching its own alignment requirement (e.g. a 4-byte `int` typically must start at an address divisible by 4):

```c
#include <stdio.h>

struct Example {
    char  a;   // 1 byte
    int   b;   // 4 bytes, needs 4-byte alignment
    char  c;   // 1 byte
};

int main(void) {
    printf("%zu\n", sizeof(struct Example)); // often 12, not 6!
    return 0;
}
```

Here the compiler inserts 3 padding bytes after `a` (so `b` starts on a 4-byte boundary) and 3 more after `c` (so the whole struct's size is a multiple of its largest member's alignment, needed for arrays of the struct to align correctly). Reordering members largest-to-smallest often reduces total padding — a real, practical optimization for memory-dense structs (e.g. `char, int, char` reordered to `int, char, char` needs only 6 bytes total).

## Passing Structs by Value Copies Them

Like any other type, passing a `struct` by value copies **all of its bytes** onto the callee's stack frame — fine for small structs, wasteful (or outright wrong, if mutation is intended) for large ones:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

typedef struct {
    int width;
    int height;
} Rectangle;

void wontChangeIt(Rectangle r) { r.width = 999; }  // mutates only the local copy

void willChangeIt(Rectangle *r) { r->width = 999; } // mutates the caller's struct

int main(void) {
    Rectangle rect = {3, 4};
    willChangeIt(&rect);
    printf("%d\n", rect.width); // 999
    return 0;
}
```

## `->` Is Shorthand for Dereference-Then-Dot

Given a pointer to a struct, `p->field` is exactly equivalent to `(*p).field` — the arrow operator exists purely for readability, since `(*p).field` requires awkward parentheses (dot binds tighter than dereference):

```c
#include <stdio.h>

typedef struct {
    int width;
    int height;
} Rectangle;

int main(void) {
    Rectangle rect = {3, 4};
    Rectangle *p = &rect;
    printf("%d\n", p->width);    // idiomatic
    printf("%d\n", (*p).width);  // equivalent, rarely written
    return 0;
}
```

## Nested Structs and Arrays of Structs

Structs can contain other structs, and arrays of structs are just as contiguous as arrays of any other type:

```c
typedef struct { int x, y; } Point;
typedef struct { Point topLeft; int width, height; } Box;

Box boxes[3] = {
    {{0, 0}, 10, 10},
    {{5, 5}, 20, 20},
    {{1, 1}, 5, 5}
};
printf("%d\n", boxes[1].topLeft.x); // 5
```

## Function Pointers as "Virtual Methods"

Embedding a function pointer *inside* a struct lets different instances carry different behavior — a hand-rolled version of what OO languages call polymorphism/virtual dispatch:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

typedef struct Shape {
    double (*area)(const struct Shape *self);
    double width, height; // reused loosely for demo purposes
} Shape;

double rectangleArea(const Shape *self) {
    return self->width * self->height;
}

double triangleArea(const Shape *self) {
    return 0.5 * self->width * self->height;
}

int main(void) {
    Shape rect = { rectangleArea, 4, 5 };
    Shape tri  = { triangleArea, 4, 5 };

    printf("%.1f\n", rect.area(&rect)); // 20.0
    printf("%.1f\n", tri.area(&tri));   // 10.0
    return 0;
}
```

`rect.area(&rect)` calls whichever function that particular struct instance was initialized with — the same struct "shape," but different behavior per instance, exactly the mechanism real C++ compilers use internally to implement virtual function tables (**vtables**).

## Encapsulation via Header/Source Split and Opaque Pointers

C has no `private` keyword, but a widely used idiom achieves the same effect: declare only a pointer to an incomplete struct type in the public header, and define the actual struct fields inside the `.c` file, invisible to callers:

```c
/* stack.h — public interface, fields hidden */
typedef struct Stack Stack; // incomplete type — size unknown outside stack.c
Stack *stack_create(void);
void   stack_push(Stack *s, int value);
void   stack_destroy(Stack *s);

/* stack.c — real definition, private to this file */
struct Stack {
    int *data;
    size_t size, capacity;
};
Stack *stack_create(void) { /* malloc and initialize */ }
```

Callers of `stack.h` can hold and pass around a `Stack *`, but cannot access `s->data` directly or even know the struct's size — they're forced through the provided functions, which is C's closest equivalent to enforced encapsulation.

## Designated Initializers Improve Clarity

For structs with several fields, naming them in the initializer avoids relying on declaration order and makes the code self-documenting, especially when only some fields need non-zero values:

```c
typedef struct { int x, y, z; int flags; } Vector;
Vector v = { .x = 1, .z = 3 }; // y and flags default to 0
```

## Worked Example: A Small "Object System" for Shapes

This program combines struct definitions, function pointers for per-instance behavior, and pointer parameters into a small polymorphism-like system:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

typedef struct Shape {
    const char *name;
    double (*area)(const struct Shape *self);
    double a, b; // generic dimensions, meaning depends on the shape
} Shape;

double rectangleArea(const Shape *self) { return self->a * self->b; }
double triangleArea(const Shape *self)  { return 0.5 * self->a * self->b; }
double circleArea(const Shape *self)    { return 3.14159 * self->a * self->a; }

void describe(const Shape *s) {
    printf("%-10s area = %.2f\n", s->name, s->area(s));
}

int main(void) {
    Shape shapes[] = {
        { "Rectangle", rectangleArea, 4, 5 },
        { "Triangle",  triangleArea,  4, 5 },
        { "Circle",    circleArea,    3, 0 },
    };

    size_t count = sizeof(shapes) / sizeof(shapes[0]);
    for (size_t i = 0; i < count; i++) {
        describe(&shapes[i]);
    }

    return 0;
}
```

`sizeof(shapes) / sizeof(shapes[0])` is a standard idiom for computing a fixed-size array's element count at compile time — it works because it divides the whole array's byte size by one element's byte size. Each `Shape` carries its own `area` function pointer, so `describe` can treat every shape uniformly while each one computes its area differently — polymorphism achieved with plain structs and function pointers, no language-level classes required.

## Recap

- `struct` groups related fields into a single named type; `typedef struct {...} Name;` is the standard idiom avoiding repeated `struct` keywords.
- C structs hold data only — "methods" are ordinary functions taking the struct (or a pointer to it) as an explicit first parameter.
- The compiler may insert padding between members for alignment; member order affects total struct size.
- Passing a struct by value copies all its bytes; pass a pointer (`Rectangle*`) when the callee should mutate the original or the struct is large.
- `p->field` is shorthand for `(*p).field` — used whenever you have a pointer to a struct.
- Embedding function pointers in a struct lets different instances carry different behavior — a hand-rolled vtable, the same technique real compilers use for virtual dispatch.
- The opaque-pointer idiom (incomplete type in the header, real fields hidden in the `.c` file) is C's closest equivalent to encapsulation/`private` fields.

