## Opening, Reading, Writing, Closing

`<stdio.h>` models files as a `FILE*` handle, opened with `fopen`, and always eventually released with `fclose`:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    FILE *out = fopen("data.txt", "w"); // "w" = write, truncating any existing file
    if (out == NULL) return 1;

    fprintf(out, "hello\nworld\n");
    fclose(out);

    char line[64];
    FILE *in = fopen("data.txt", "r"); // "r" = read
    if (in == NULL) return 1;

    while (fgets(line, sizeof(line), in)) {
        printf("%s", line);
    }
    fclose(in);
    return 0;
}
```

## Common `fopen` Modes

- `"r"` — read (file must already exist)
- `"w"` — write, **truncating** the file to zero length if it exists (or creating it)
- `"a"` — append, writing always goes to the end
- `"r+"`/`"w+"`/`"a+"` — the `+` adds the opposite capability (reading while writing, etc.)
- Add `"b"` (e.g. `"rb"`, `"wb"`) for binary mode, relevant on some platforms (notably Windows) where text mode translates line endings.

**Always check `fopen`'s return value for `NULL`** before using the handle — a missing file, wrong permissions, or a full disk are all ordinary, expected failure modes, not exceptional edge cases.

## Reading and Writing Beyond Text Lines

- `fgets(buf, size, f)` — reads one line, bounded by `size` (safe, like the input module covered).
- `fprintf(f, "...", ...)` — writes formatted text, just like `printf` but to a file.
- `fread(buf, elemSize, count, f)` / `fwrite(buf, elemSize, count, f)` — read/write raw binary data in bulk, returning the number of complete elements actually transferred (which can be less than requested at end-of-file or on error).

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int nums[5] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
FILE *f = fopen("nums.bin", "wb");
fwrite(nums, sizeof(int), 5, f);
fclose(f);

int loaded[5];
f = fopen("nums.bin", "rb");
size_t got = fread(loaded, sizeof(int), 5, f);
fclose(f);
printf("read %zu ints\n", got);
```

## Other Key Standard Headers

- `<stdlib.h>` — `malloc`/`free`, `atoi`/`atof` (string-to-number), `exit`, `qsort`, `rand`.
- `<string.h>` — `strlen`, `strcpy`, `strcmp`, `strcat`, `memcpy`, `memset`.
- `<math.h>` — `sqrt`, `pow`, `fabs`, `floor`/`ceil`; on Linux/gcc these typically require linking the math library explicitly with `-lm`.
- `<ctype.h>` — `isalpha`, `isdigit`, `isspace`, `toupper`/`tolower`, for classifying/transforming individual characters.

## Worked Example: A Small Logging Header + File Writer

This combines a custom header with an include guard, conditional-compilation macros, and file I/O into one small, realistic "logging utility" — showing how these pieces fit together in real multi-file C projects (represented here in one file with comments marking the header boundary):

```c
/* ---- logger.h (in a real project, this is a separate file) ---- */
#ifndef LOGGER_H
#define LOGGER_H

#include <stdio.h>

#ifdef DEBUG_BUILD
    #define LOG_DEBUG(msg) fprintf(stderr, "[DEBUG] %s\n", (msg))
#else
    #define LOG_DEBUG(msg) /* compiled away entirely in release builds */
#endif

static inline void logToFile(FILE *f, const char *msg) {
    if (f == NULL) return;
    fprintf(f, "%s\n", msg);
    fflush(f);
}

#endif /* LOGGER_H */
/* ---- end logger.h ---- */

#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    LOG_DEBUG("starting up"); // prints only if DEBUG_BUILD is defined

    FILE *log = fopen("app.log", "a"); // append mode — never truncates old entries
    if (log == NULL) {
        fprintf(stderr, "could not open log file\n");
        return 1;
    }

    logToFile(log, "application started");
    logToFile(log, "processing complete");
    fclose(log);

    /* read it back to confirm */
    FILE *check = fopen("app.log", "r");
    if (check != NULL) {
        char line[128];
        while (fgets(line, sizeof(line), check)) {
            printf("%s", line);
        }
        fclose(check);
    }

    return 0;
}
```

Compiling with `-DDEBUG_BUILD` would define that macro and enable `LOG_DEBUG`'s output; omitting it compiles the debug logging away entirely, at zero runtime cost — a real, common technique for keeping diagnostic logging out of release binaries.

## Recap

- `fopen`/`fprintf`/`fgets`/`fread`/`fwrite`/`fclose` form the standard file I/O toolkit; always check `fopen`'s return for `NULL`.
- `feof`/`ferror` distinguish "reached the end" from "an actual error occurred" after a short read.
- The preprocessor runs before compilation as pure text substitution; `#include`, `#define`, and `#ifdef`/`#endif` are its core directives.
- Include guards (`#ifndef`/`#define`/`#endif`, or `#pragma once`) prevent a header's contents from being pasted in more than once per file.
- Headers should hold only declarations; actual definitions belong in exactly one `.c` file to avoid linker errors.
- Always fully parenthesize macro parameters and their overall expansion — unparenthesized macros interact badly with operator precedence.
- Macro arguments can be evaluated multiple times; prefer `inline` functions over macros whenever arguments might have side effects.
- `math.h` functions typically require linking the math library (`-lm`) on Linux/gcc.

